# Agent Wolf — local topology config.
#
# START ORANGE FIRST. Agent Wolf's compose file joins Orange's compose
# network as EXTERNAL (agent-orange_default) and shares Orange's DinD
# container's network namespace — both must already exist. See
# design/2026-08-20-agent-wolf.md § "Local topology and networking"
# (agent-orange repo) for why: agentd shares DinD's netns in the standalone
# stack, so a naive wolf-api sitting on the ordinary compose network would be
# unreachable from session containers.
#
#   cd ../agent-orange && cp .env.example .env && docker compose up --build
#   cd ../agent-wolf   && cp .env.example .env && docker compose up --build

# ── Topology (docker-compose.yml only — read by Docker, not by wolf-api) ─────

# Name of Agent Orange's dind container. wolf-api runs with
# `network_mode: "container:${ORANGE_DIND_CONTAINER}"`, so it shares that
# container's network namespace: it reaches agentd at http://localhost:8099
# (same netns) and is reachable FROM session containers at
# http://172.17.0.1:${WOLF_API_PORT} (the DinD bridge gateway — the address
# nested session containers actually resolve). Default matches the compose
# project name "agent-orange" (the directory name) with the default
# `docker compose` naming: <project>-<service>-<replica>.
ORANGE_DIND_CONTAINER=agent-orange-dind-1

# Port wolf-web (nginx) publishes on the host. Open http://localhost:$WOLF_WEB_PORT.
WOLF_WEB_PORT=8081

# Port wolf-api listens on, INSIDE the shared dind netns (not a host port —
# wolf-api publishes no host port of its own; only wolf-web does). Read by
# wolf-api itself (api/src/config.ts) to bind its HTTP server, and by
# wolf-web's nginx template to build its proxy_pass target
# (http://dind:${WOLF_API_PORT}), and by session containers' MCP config
# (http://172.17.0.1:${WOLF_API_PORT}/mcp, i.e. WOLF_MCP_URL below).
WOLF_API_PORT=8100

# ── wolf-api process config (api/src/config.ts) ───────────────────────────────

# The value written into the project's MCP config so a session container can
# reach wolf-api's /mcp endpoint. In THIS compose stack that is the DinD
# bridge address, never a compose service name (nested containers cannot
# resolve compose DNS) and never localhost (that would resolve inside the
# session container itself). In a real (non-compose) deployment both
# services are ordinary hosts and this is just wolf-api's normal /mcp URL —
# the variable exists so nothing hard-codes either form.
#
# Read directly by api/src/config.ts (resolveMcpUrl), not just by Docker:
# READ THIS BEFORE UNCOMMENTING. Left unset (the default here), wolf-api
# DISCOVERS the DinD bridge address itself at boot — reads the default
# route from inside DinD's shared network namespace and falls back to the
# literal 172.17.0.1 only if that fails — because that address is a Docker
# default, not a constant: it becomes e.g. 172.18.0.1 whenever the
# 172.17.0.0/16 subnet is already taken on the host. Setting this variable
# explicitly WINS OUTRIGHT over discovery, so only set it if you know the
# gateway will not move (a real, non-compose deployment, where both
# services are ordinary hosts) — in the compose stack, leave it unset and
# let wolf-api discover it; check the "resolved WOLF_MCP_URL" boot log line
# to see which path was used.
# WOLF_MCP_URL=http://172.17.0.1:8100/mcp

# pino log level: fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace | silent.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# development | production | test.
NODE_ENV=production

# ── Market data + the wolf MCP server (api/src/config.ts, api/src/mcp/) ──────

# The credential a session container presents to wolf-api's /mcp endpoint,
# in the BARE header `X-Wolf-Mcp-Token: <token>` — no `Bearer` prefix, ever:
# Orange stores an MCP header value as a whole-value ${VAR} reference or a
# literal, never a mix, so "Bearer ${WOLF_MCP_TOKEN}" cannot be stored at
# all. It is a DIFFERENT credential from WOLF_API_KEY (which authenticates
# wolf-api TO Orange) and it must be the same value on both sides: here, and
# in the `wolf` project's MCP config that reaches the container through
# Orange's AGENTKIT_MCP_ENV.
#
# REQUIRED: wolf-api refuses to boot without it rather than serve its
# market-data tools unauthenticated. 32-128 characters of [A-Za-z0-9_-]
# (URL-safe base64) — no spaces, quotes or `$`, which are how a value
# travelling through a shell export, Docker and Orange's ${VAR}
# interpolation breaks silently. Generate one, and NEVER commit the value:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '='
WOLF_MCP_TOKEN=

# The HMAC key that signs /series/download URLs (`series_fetch` mints them;
# the route verifies them). At least 32 characters.
#
# OPTIONAL: left unset, wolf-api generates a fresh random secret at boot.
# That is a real default, not a weak one — there is no shared constant to
# leak. The only consequence is that URLs minted before a restart stop
# verifying after it, which the short TTL below makes near-harmless. Set it
# only if more than one wolf-api process must honour each other's URLs.
# WOLF_SERIES_TOKEN_SECRET=

# How long a minted /series/download URL stays valid, in whole SECONDS
# (1-3600, default 300). Keep it short: the URL carries its token into the
# model's context, the persisted transcript and every worker.finished
# subscriber — the same caveat the design states for Orange's dataset_get.
# WOLF_SERIES_URL_TTL_SECONDS=300

# FRED (St. Louis Fed) API key, for the macro half of the market-data tools.
# Free and instant from https://fredaccount.stlouisfed.org/apikeys — the
# KEYED JSON API is the only one with a /fred/series/search endpoint, which
# series_search needs.
#
# OPTIONAL: unset, wolf-api still boots and Stooq (daily US equity/ETF
# closes) still works; the FRED connector is built lazily, so the first FRED
# tool call answers with a `misconfigured` error naming this variable
# instead. Never commit the value.
# FRED_API_KEY=

# TTL of the in-process market-data cache, in whole SECONDS (default 3600).
# Keyed by (source, id, from, to); a warm entry means a /series/download
# issues zero upstream HTTP requests. Note the _SECONDS suffix: every
# duration variable in this codebase holds a plain integer count of seconds,
# never a "5m"-style string.
# WOLF_MARKETDATA_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=3600
